<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarization requires.

  This file started EMPTY on purpose. zed's own entitlements
  (crates/zed/resources/zed.entitlements in the pinned checkout) are not a
  template: most of its entries — camera, audio input, address book, calendars,
  location, photos — exist for collab and screen-sharing features that this app
  deliberately keeps inert via BlockedHttpClient (workspace_host.rs:72), and its
  files.user-selected.read-write entries are App Sandbox keys that do nothing in
  an unsandboxed app. Copying them would request privacy-sensitive entitlements
  we never use and leave nobody able to explain any line.

  Add an entitlement ONLY when a hardened-runtime build demonstrably fails
  without it, and record the observed failure here. Keep the keys themselves in
  a bare dict: AMFI's plist parser rejects comments inside the dict element, so
  the rationale lives in this block instead.

  App Sandbox is NOT adopted: notarization does not require it, and it is
  incompatible with spawning agents, shells, and git across arbitrary worktrees.


  com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
  ======================================================
  Required by the tree-sitter (0.26.11) to wasmtime (36) path.

  The guess this file used to carry, that the JIT might never execute because
  we bundle native grammars, was wrong. zed's language::with_parser
  (crates/language/src/language.rs:133 in the pinned checkout) calls
  set_wasm_store(WasmStore::new(..)) unconditionally for every Parser it
  constructs, so the FIRST syntax-highlighted parse of ANY file instantiates a
  wasmtime module. Bundling native grammars does not avoid it.

  Observed without this key: opening a Rust file in the editor pane killed the
  app instantly. Not a panic, a SIGKILL from the kernel:

      exception:   EXC_BAD_ACCESS, SIGKILL (Code Signature Invalid)
      termination: CODESIGNING, indicator "Invalid Page"
      thread 13:   WasmStore::new to ts_wasm_store_new
                   to wasmtime_instance_new to an anonymous r-x page

  allow-jit is deliberately NOT paired with this. It only covers regions mapped
  with MAP_JIT, and wasmtime never asks for one: it mmaps ordinary anonymous
  memory and mprotects it to READ|EXEC (wasmtime-36.0.13
  src/runtime/vm/sys/unix/mmap.rs:147-163). Measured on the crashing path,
  signed with the hardened runtime: empty dict gave SIGKILL; allow-jit alone
  gave SIGKILL; this key alone ran clean; both ran clean. allow-jit would be an
  entitlement we request and never use, so it stays out.

  Design: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-04-signing-notarization-design.md
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
